Postgres full-text search, not a separate search service — 38k rows and slow growth don't justify running Elasticsearch/Algolia alongside RDS.

Add a generated `tsvector` column over `name || description`, GIN-indexed, and query it with `websearch_to_tsquery` (handles multi-word input the way users type it) ranked by `ts_rank`:

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search_vector tsvector
  GENERATED ALWAYS AS (to_tsvector('english', name || ' ' || description)) STORED;
CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING GIN (search_vector);
```

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents
     FROM products
     WHERE active AND search_vector @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)
     ORDER BY ts_rank(search_vector, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)) DESC
     LIMIT 50`,
    [req.query.q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

The one real fork: this gives whole-word/stemmed matching, not typo tolerance ("shirtt" won't match "shirt"). If fuzzy/partial matching turns out to matter, that's `pg_trgm` + a GIN trigram index layered on top — worth prototyping the plain FTS version first and seeing if real queries need it.

